---
import EditorialLayout from "../layouts/EditorialLayout.astro";
import "../styles/pages/sportswear-manufacturer.css";
---

<EditorialLayout
  title={"Custom Sportswear Manufacturer for Private Label Brands | YAUUM"}
  description={"Develop custom sportswear for private label brands. Review product styles, fabrics, fit, branding, MOQ, sampling, production, quality checks, and packing."}
  canonical="https://yauum.com/sportswear-manufacturer/"
  navCurrent={["/production/", "/sportswear-manufacturer/"]}
  showMobileInquiry
>
<!-- ============ HERO ============ -->
<section class="pd-hero" aria-labelledby="sw-title" data-od-id="sw-hero">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-hero-grid">
      <div class="pd-hero-copy">
        <p class="label">B2B / PRIVATE LABEL / SAMPLE-FIRST</p>
        <h1 id="sw-title">Custom Sportswear Manufacturer for Private Label Brands</h1>
        <p class="lead pd-hero-lead">Customize a sportswear range around the product, activity, fit, fabric, construction, branding, and order requirements your market needs. YAUUM uses a sample-first process so your team can review the garment, record changes, and approve a reference before bulk production.</p>
        <p class="sw-hero-note">Start with a tech pack, reference garment, product photo, or early concept. Define the wearer, use, quantity, size split, artwork, packing, destination, and target timing to make the first review and quotation more useful.</p>
        <div class="sw-hero-actions">
          <a class="button" href="/contact-us/">Start your sportswear project</a>
          <a class="link" href="#sportswear-products">Review product options</a>
        </div>
        <div class="sw-signals" aria-label="Sportswear development steps">
          <span><strong>01</strong> Product and use</span>
          <span><strong>02</strong> Sample and approval</span>
          <span><strong>03</strong> Bulk and handoff</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="pd-hero-media">
        <div class="sw-ph sw-ph--hero" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for a verified private label sportswear range">
          <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
          <div class="sw-ph-copy">
            <strong>Private label sportswear range</strong>
            <small>Needed: verified training top, shorts, joggers, and track layer from confirmed projects.</small>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ SECTION INDEX ============ -->
<nav class="pd-index" aria-label="Sportswear page sections" data-od-id="page-section-index">
  <div class="shell">
    <a href="#sportswear-products"><span class="pd-num">01</span>Products</a>
    <a href="#sportswear-styles"><span class="pd-num">02</span>Styles</a>
    <a href="#sportswear-fabric"><span class="pd-num">03</span>Fabric</a>
    <a href="#sportswear-fit"><span class="pd-num">04</span>Fit</a>
    <a href="#sportswear-details"><span class="pd-num">05</span>Details</a>
    <a href="#sportswear-branding"><span class="pd-num">06</span>Branding</a>
    <a href="#sportswear-moq"><span class="pd-num">07</span>MOQ</a>
    <a href="#sportswear-sample"><span class="pd-num">08</span>Sample</a>
    <a href="#sportswear-production"><span class="pd-num">09</span>Production</a>
    <a href="#sportswear-quality"><span class="pd-num">10</span>Quality</a>
    <a href="#sportswear-faq"><span class="pd-num">11</span>FAQ</a>
    <a href="#sportswear-brief"><span class="pd-num">12</span>Brief</a>
  </div>
</nav>

<!-- ============ 01 PRODUCTS ============ -->
<section class="section" id="sportswear-products" aria-labelledby="sportswear-products-title" data-od-id="sw-products">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label">01 / Products</p>
        <h2 id="sportswear-products-title">What Sportswear Products Do You Want to Customize?</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">Start with the garment you want to sell. Training tops, running shorts, joggers, track jackets, and team jerseys create different fit, fabric, artwork, and quantity decisions. Choose the product family before specifying details.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-grid sw-grid--2">
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-num">01</span>
        <h3>Training Tops and Base Layers</h3>
        <p>Possible directions include training T-shirts, tanks, compression tops, and base layers. State the activity, layering need, sleeve, neckline, body length, fit, and coverage so the sample has a clear purpose.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-num">02</span>
        <h3>Sports Shorts, Joggers, and Track Pants</h3>
        <p>For bottoms, specify the activity first. A running short may prioritize low bulk and secure storage; a jogger may need a defined leg shape. Include rise, inseam, waistband, liner, pockets, leg opening, and mobility.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-num">03</span>
        <h3>Track Jackets and Coordinated Sets</h3>
        <p>Plan a track jacket or tracksuit as one coordinated product. Align jacket length, pant shape, panels, colors, trims, branding, and sizes. If sold separately, make sure each garment also works alone.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-num">04</span>
        <h3>Team Jerseys and Sport-Specific Directions</h3>
        <p>Teamwear may need names, numbers, sponsor artwork, club colors, and consistent placement. Identify the sport, uniform pieces, roster variations, and reorder expectations for decoration and order review.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-media sw-media--4" data-reveal>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified training tops and base layers">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Training tops and base layers</strong><small>Needed: verified front-and-back views of a confirmed training top or base-layer direction.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified sportswear bottoms">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Shorts, joggers, and track pants</strong><small>Needed: verified product views showing the confirmed sportswear bottom direction and key silhouette.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified track jackets or sets">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Track jackets and coordinated sets</strong><small>Needed: a verified jacket or coordinated set, shown without implying every listed option is standard.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified teamwear or sport-specific products">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Team jerseys and sport-specific pieces</strong><small>Needed: a verified jersey or sport-specific product with any buyer or team identity removed before publication.</small></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 02 STYLES ============ -->
<section class="section band-dark" id="sportswear-styles" aria-labelledby="sportswear-styles-title" data-od-id="sw-styles">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label label--onDark">02 / Styles</p>
        <h2 id="sportswear-styles-title">Explore Sportswear Styles for Your Collection</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">Next, define the collection direction. A T-shirt or short changes when designed for gym training, running, team use, or active lifestyle retail. This choice guides silhouette, material, construction, color, and branding.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-grid sw-grid--3">
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-num">01</span>
        <h3>Gym and Training Styles</h3>
        <p>For gym or general training, choose a supportive, lightweight, relaxed, or layered direction. Identify the main movements, wearer, coverage, storage needs, and whether tops and bottoms share one visual system.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-num">02</span>
        <h3>Running and Outdoor Training Styles</h3>
        <p>Running styles may prioritize low bulk, ventilation, secure pockets, movement, and visibility details. Define the training context, climate, layering plan, and selling season before selecting shape and features.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-num">03</span>
        <h3>Teamwear, Club, and Active Lifestyle Styles</h3>
        <p>Teamwear may need coordinated colors, names, numbers, and repeatable artwork. Active lifestyle ranges may prioritize easy silhouettes and matching sets. State whether the range is for competition, club use, warm-up, travel, or retail.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-media" data-reveal>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified gym and training styles">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Gym and training direction</strong><small>Needed: a verified training look showing the intended fit, layering, and product relationship.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified running or outdoor styles">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Running and outdoor training direction</strong><small>Needed: a verified running or outdoor-training look with only confirmed details shown.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified teamwear or active lifestyle styles">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Teamwear and active lifestyle direction</strong><small>Needed: a verified coordinated look for team, club, warm-up, travel, or active lifestyle use.</small></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 03 FABRIC ============ -->
<section class="section band-alt" id="sportswear-fabric" aria-labelledby="sportswear-fabric-title" data-od-id="sw-fabric">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label">03 / Fabric</p>
        <h2 id="sportswear-fabric-title">Choose Fabric for Movement, Comfort, and the Intended Use</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">Choose fabric against the garment, activity, climate, hand feel, opacity, stretch, decoration, and intended shape. A compression top and a relaxed warm-up pant require different behavior; a generic performance label does not settle the decision.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-spec">
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-spec-num">01</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Knits for Tops, Jerseys, and Stretch Styles</h3>
          <p>Candidate knits include polyester jersey, interlock, polyester-spandex blends, cotton blends, and mesh. Compare face and reverse, stretch, recovery, weight, drape, hand feel, and color. For close fits, also review opacity, support, and skin feel.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-spec-num">02</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Wovens for Shorts, Pants, and Lightweight Layers</h3>
          <p>Possible woven directions include lightweight polyester, nylon, stretch woven, and ripstop. Select against the product and silhouette, then check noise, drape, stretch, skin contact, pocket load, and compatibility with waistbands, trims, panels, and decoration.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-spec-num">03</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Define Performance Targets Before Using Performance Claims</h3>
          <p>If moisture management, quick drying, breathability, UV protection, water resistance, or another function matters, define the target and test method. A fabric name does not prove performance; publish a claim only after the requirement and supporting result are confirmed.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-media" data-reveal>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified knit and jersey references">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Knit and jersey surfaces</strong><small>Needed: verified face, reverse, edge, and drape views of confirmed knit or jersey candidates.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified mesh or stretch references">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Mesh and stretch structures</strong><small>Needed: verified close-ups showing the structure and hand feel without adding unconfirmed performance claims.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified woven fabric references">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Woven and lightweight layers</strong><small>Needed: verified woven or lightweight-layer swatches with the selected composition confirmed before publication.</small></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 04 FIT ============ -->
<section class="section" id="sportswear-fit" aria-labelledby="sportswear-fit-title" data-od-id="sw-fit">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label">04 / Fit</p>
        <h2 id="sportswear-fit-title">Get the Athletic Fit Right Before Sampling</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">Judge sportswear on the body and in motion. Choose the intended fit, sample size, and measurements that control movement and appearance. This is more useful than relying on labels such as slim, athletic, or relaxed.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-stack">
      <article class="sw-stack-row" data-reveal>
        <div class="sw-stack-lead"><span class="sw-stack-num">01</span><h3>Match the Fit to the Customer and Activity</h3></div>
        <p>Describe the wearer, activity, layering needs, and silhouette. Compression, slim, athletic, regular, or relaxed are starting points, not specifications. Reference measurements or a physical garment make chest, waist, seat, thigh, sleeve, and body proportions clearer.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-stack-row" data-reveal>
        <div class="sw-stack-lead"><span class="sw-stack-num">02</span><h3>Identify the Measurements That Control the Garment</h3></div>
        <p>For tops, define chest, shoulder, armhole, sleeve, body length, and openings. For bottoms, define waist, hip, rise, thigh, inseam, outseam, and leg opening. Mark any point critical to coverage, movement, or styling.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-stack-row" data-reveal>
        <div class="sw-stack-lead"><span class="sw-stack-num">03</span><h3>Review the Full Size Range, Not Only One Sample</h3></div>
        <p>A sample shows one size; the order must work across the range. Provide the size chart, grading rules, and fit model information where available. Confirm measurements, tolerances, and size relationships before bulk rather than assuming the sample scales automatically.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-media" data-reveal>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified sportswear fit views">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Front, side, and back fit</strong><small>Needed: verified front, side, and back views of one confirmed sample with buyer-identifying information removed.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified sportswear movement review">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Movement review</strong><small>Needed: verified on-body movement views relevant to the garment, without presenting them as a performance test result.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified sportswear measurement review">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Measurement and size review</strong><small>Needed: a verified flat measurement or size-review scene with confidential data removed.</small></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 05 DETAILS ============ -->
<section class="section band-alt" id="sportswear-details" aria-labelledby="sportswear-details-title" data-od-id="sw-details">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label">05 / Details</p>
        <h2 id="sportswear-details-title">Build in the Details That Make the Garment Work</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">Construction should solve a wearer need. Mark where movement, ventilation, storage, adjustment, or reinforcement matters. Every added component affects sampling, cost, and production.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-grid sw-grid--2">
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-num">01</span>
        <h3>Sleeves, Panels, Gussets, and Seams</h3>
        <p>Possible directions include raglan or set-in sleeves, mesh zones, side panels, and gussets. Mark their shapes and positions. Define any required flat seams, bonded areas, reinforcement, or reduced-friction construction.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-num">02</span>
        <h3>Waistbands, Drawcords, Liners, and Openings</h3>
        <p>For bottoms, define waistband depth, elastic behavior, drawcord position, liner, rise, inseam, and leg opening. For tops, confirm neck, cuff, hem, and front opening. Show how each should fit during relevant movement or layering.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-num">03</span>
        <h3>Pockets and Secure Storage</h3>
        <p>State what each pocket must hold and where it should be reached. Side, back, hidden, and zippered pockets affect construction and comfort. Define opening size, bag depth, closure, placement, and reinforcement for the intended item.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-num">04</span>
        <h3>Trims, Reflective Details, and Wearer Contact</h3>
        <p>List each zipper, puller, elastic, cord, stopper, binding, tape, and reflective component. Confirm color, size, finish, placement, and skin contact. Treat reflective material as a specified component, not an automatic safety guarantee.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-media" data-reveal>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified sportswear construction">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Panels, gussets, and seams</strong><small>Needed: verified close-ups of confirmed panel, gusset, seam, or sleeve construction.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified sportswear waist and opening details">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Waistbands, drawcords, and openings</strong><small>Needed: verified details of the selected waistband, drawcord, liner, cuff, hem, or opening.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified sportswear functional details">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Pockets, trims, and reflective details</strong><small>Needed: verified pocket, zipper, trim, binding, or reflective-component details with project scope confirmed.</small></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 06 BRANDING ============ -->
<section class="section band-dark" id="sportswear-branding" aria-labelledby="sportswear-branding-title" data-od-id="sw-branding">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label label--onDark">06 / Branding</p>
        <h2 id="sportswear-branding-title">Make the Branding Work With the Fabric and the Order</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">Artwork is part of the specification. Send its source file, colors, dimensions, position, coverage, and intended appearance. The suitable route depends on fabric, stretch, construction, quantity, and expected result.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-grid sw-grid--2">
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-num">01</span>
        <h3>Sublimation and All-Over Artwork</h3>
        <p>Sublimation may suit some polyester-based styles with large graphics, numbers, or color transitions. Confirm panels, artwork scale, color references, seam alignment, and size variation. Check material suitability and finished color for the project.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-num">02</span>
        <h3>Prints, Transfers, and Reflective Graphics</h3>
        <p>Screen print, heat transfer, DTF, DTG, silicone, and reflective graphics are candidate methods. Review fabric surface, stretch, artwork detail, hand feel, coverage, placement, and quantity. Check the selected method during sampling.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-num">03</span>
        <h3>Embroidery, Badges, and Placement</h3>
        <p>Embroidery and badges add weight, stiffness, backing, and possible skin contact. Define size, materials, position, edge finish, and reverse-side expectation. Review distortion and comfort before approving lightweight or high-stretch placements.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-num">04</span>
        <h3>Labels, Hangtags, Barcodes, and Packing Identity</h3>
        <p>Private label inputs can include main, size, care, or heat-transfer labels, hangtags, stickers, barcodes, bags, and carton marks. Provide final files, wording, dimensions, materials, attachment points, and market requirements for sample and packing review.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-media sw-media--4" data-reveal>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified sublimation or all-over artwork">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Sublimation and all-over artwork</strong><small>Needed: a verified project example showing confirmed artwork coverage, panel alignment, and material suitability.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified sportswear print or transfer details">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Prints, transfers, and reflective graphics</strong><small>Needed: verified decoration close-ups on relevant materials, with the selected method confirmed per project.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified sportswear embroidery or badges">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Embroidery and badges</strong><small>Needed: verified embroidery or badge placement showing size, backing, edge, and wearer-contact considerations.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified sportswear labels and packing">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Labels, hangtags, and packing identity</strong><small>Needed: verified private-label components and packing identity with buyer data removed before publication.</small></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 07 MOQ ============ -->
<section class="section" id="sportswear-moq" aria-labelledby="sportswear-moq-title" data-od-id="sw-moq">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label">07 / MOQ</p>
        <h2 id="sportswear-moq-title">What Will Determine the MOQ and Quote for Your Sportswear?</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">A useful MOQ needs a defined product. Order structure, fabric, colors, sizes, decoration, trims, labels, packing, and development work affect how a project can be arranged. A complete brief makes the quotation more specific.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-cards sw-cards--3">
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-card-num">01</span>
        <h3>Style, Color, Size, and Quantity Breakdown</h3>
        <p>State total quantity and the split by style, color, and size. A coordinated set differs from several unrelated pieces sharing a logo. Include expected repeat orders, replacements, or personalized team units when the order structure is reviewed.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-card-num">02</span>
        <h3>Fabric, Decoration, and Custom Component Factors</h3>
        <p>Custom-dyed fabric, special trims, wide artwork, personalized names, multiple print versions, and branded packing can affect minimums and cost. Mark essential elements and acceptable alternatives so the quotation can separate the core product from optional details.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal>
        <span class="sw-card-num">03</span>
        <h3>What to Send for a More Useful Quote</h3>
        <p>Send the product, tech pack or reference, fabric direction, colors, sizes, quantity split, artwork, labels, packing, destination, target timing, and open decisions. Confirm MOQ, price, sample terms, and production timing for that specification.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-media sw-media--1" data-reveal>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for a verified sportswear quote brief">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Quantity and specification breakdown</strong><small>Needed: a verified project brief or assortment matrix with prices, customer names, and unconfirmed quantities removed.</small></div>
      </div>
    </div>
