</section>

<!-- ============ 08 SAMPLE ============ -->
<section class="section band-alt" id="sportswear-sample" aria-labelledby="sportswear-sample-title" data-od-id="sw-sample">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label">08 / Sample</p>
        <h2 id="sportswear-sample-title">Test the Sample in Motion Before Bulk Production</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">The sample makes assumptions visible. Put it on the intended wearer, move in it, measure it, inspect the details, and compare it with the brief. Record specific changes instead of broad feedback.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-checkrows">
      <article class="sw-checkrow" data-reveal>
        <div class="sw-check-lead"><span class="sw-check-num">01</span><h3>Review Fit During the Intended Movements</h3></div>
        <p>Use movements relevant to the garment: lifting, bending, squatting, running, stretching, or layering. Check restriction, riding up, gaping, twisting, pressure, coverage, and pocket movement. Record the wearer, sample size, action, and issue.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-checkrow" data-reveal>
        <div class="sw-check-lead"><span class="sw-check-num">02</span><h3>Check Fabric, Construction, and Branding Together</h3></div>
        <p>Review stretch, recovery, opacity, surface, seam comfort, panel position, waistband, closures, pockets, and decoration on one sample. Consider linked changes: moving a panel affects artwork placement, while changing fabric may alter fit and decoration behavior.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-checkrow" data-reveal>
        <div class="sw-check-lead"><span class="sw-check-num">03</span><h3>Turn Comments Into an Approved Reference</h3></div>
        <p>Mark revisions with a measurement, location, photo, or artwork reference. Keep the tech pack, comments, and physical sample aligned. The approved sample and specification become the bulk reference; unresolved points should remain visible.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-media" data-reveal>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified sportswear motion review">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Motion and on-body review</strong><small>Needed: verified motion-check views tied to the intended garment use, not a general performance guarantee.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified sportswear sample details">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Fit, fabric, and branding review</strong><small>Needed: a verified sample-review scene showing construction, fabric, and decoration together.</small></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sw-ph" role="img" aria-label="Pending image slot for verified sportswear approval reference">
        <div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">IMAGE PLACEHOLDER</span></div>
        <div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Comments and approved reference</strong><small>Needed: a verified revision or approval record with confidential buyer information removed.</small></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 09 PRODUCTION ============ -->
<section class="section" id="sportswear-production" aria-labelledby="sportswear-production-title" data-od-id="sw-production">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label">09 / Production</p>
        <h2 id="sportswear-production-title">From Approved Sample to Packed Sportswear: What Happens Next?</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">Bulk work should follow the approved sample, specification, and order. The production flow keeps materials, measurements, workmanship, branding, quantities, and packing connected to the buyer-approved reference.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-stack">
      <article class="sw-stack-row" data-reveal>
        <div class="sw-stack-lead"><span class="sw-stack-num">01</span><h3>Confirm the Order Reference Before Production</h3></div>
        <p>Align the approved sample, current specification, colors, materials, sizes, artwork, labels, packing, and quantities. Resolve version conflicts before work proceeds so the buyer and production team use the same product definition.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-stack-row" data-reveal>
        <div class="sw-stack-lead"><span class="sw-stack-num">02</span><h3>Prepare Materials, Cutting, Sewing, and Decoration</h3></div>
        <p>Depending on the garment, stages can include material preparation, cutting, panel preparation, sewing, decoration, finishing, and pressing. Keep approved measurements, panel alignment, stretch areas, and artwork placement visible at the relevant stage.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-stack-row" data-reveal>
        <div class="sw-stack-lead"><span class="sw-stack-num">03</span><h3>Keep Useful Records at the Right Stage</h3></div>
        <p>Buyers can request stage-specific photos, measurements, inspection information, packing details, or related documents. Agree what is needed and when. A short record tied to a decision is more useful than unlabelled photos received after completion.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sw-stack-row" data-reveal>
        <div class="sw-stack-lead"><span class="sw-stack-num">04</span><h3>Finish, Fold, Pack, and Prepare for Shipment</h3></div>
        <p>Confirm finishing, folding, individual packing, barcode or sticker placement, carton quantity, assortment, and carton marks. Agree shipping documents, destination requirements, and handoff responsibilities. Packing is part of the product handoff.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-media" data-reveal>
